y and dilapidated, with crumbling concrete walls, makeshift structures, and debris. The same subtitle from Frame 7 continues: “CC: Being killed by a warlord, being killed by poverty and hunger and being killed by American bullet.”\n\n- **Frame 9-11:** The camera continues to follow the pair as they walk. A third man in a blue shirt walks ahead of them. The setting remains bleak — broken walls, a rusted barrel, and sparse vegetation. The subtitle remains unchanged, reinforcing the connection between the interview’s message and the visual reality on the ground.\n\n- **Frame 12-13:** The camera angle shifts slightly, showing more of the surroundings. Another person in a purple shirt enters the frame from the left. The boy and the man in plaid continue walking, still holding hands. The subtitle remains.\n\n- **Frame 14:** A new subtitle appears: “It’s rather the American bullet, and with the chance of winning also something, sometime.” The man in plaid gestures with his free hand as he walks. The scene continues to emphasize the harsh living conditions.\n\n- **Frame 15-16:** The man in plaid turns slightly, still walking, while the boy looks ahead. The man in purple walks ahead of them. The environment remains consistent — dusty, broken, and impoverished. The subtitle continues through Frame 16.\n\n---\n\n**Answer to Your Specific Question:**\n\n> What happens immediately after the interview with Mohamed Abshir Waldo? Does it cut to a signature ceremony, crew shooting, press conference, or examination room?\n\n**No.** Immediately after the interview with Mohamed Abshir Waldo (ending at Frame 7), the video **cuts to a scene in a desert village**, showing a man and a young boy walking through a dilapidated, impoverished area. This is **not** a signature ceremony, crew shooting, press conference, or examination room. Instead, it is a **visual illustration of the human consequences** of the issues Waldo is discussing — poverty, violence, and the impact of foreign interventi